"Some new results on permutation polynomials over finite fields (https://arxiv.org/abs/1506.05525)\nciteturn0academia12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.4 years ago; Authors: Jingxue Ma, Tao Zhang, Tao Feng, Gennian Ge ... In this paper, four classes of monomial complete permutation polynomials and one class of trinomial complete permutation polynomials are presented, one of which confirms a conjecture proposed by Wu et al.\nTitle: Some new results on permutation polynomials over finite fields\nAuthors: Jingxue Ma, Tao Zhang, Tao Feng, Gennian Ge\nDate: Thu Jun 18 01:15:34 2015\n\nPermutation polynomials over finite fields constitute an active research area and have applications in many areas of science and engineering. In this paper, four classes of monomial complete permutation polynomials and one class of trinomial complete permutation polynomials are presented, one of which confirms a conjecture proposed by Wu et al. (Sci. China Math., to appear. Doi: 10.1007/s11425-014-4964-2). Furthermore, we give two classes of trinomial permutation polynomials, and make some progress on a conjecture about the differential uniformity of power permutation polynomials proposed by Blondeau et al. (Int. J. Inf. Coding Theory, 2010, 1, pp. 149-170).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFurther results on complete permutation monomials over finite fields (https://doi.org/10.1016%2Fj.ffa.2019.01.003)\nciteturn0search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Furthermore, the authors wrote “As a corollary, a conjecture by Wu, Li, Helleseth and Zhang is proven in odd characteristic”. ... In this paper, we construct several new classes of complete permutation monomials of {\\mathbb{F}}_{q} using the AGW criterion, when is either a Dickson permutation polynomial or a degree p exceptional polynomial.\n## Conjecture 4.18 in [42]\n\nLet be a prime such that . Let and , and . Then there exists such that is a Dickson polynomial of degree over {\\mathbb{F}}_{{p}^{k}}.\n\nUsing the classification of exceptional polynomials, Bartoli et al. [8] classified complete permutation monomials of degree over {\\mathbb{F}}_{{q}^{n}}, where q is odd, is prime and . Furthermore, the authors wrote “As a corollary, a conjecture by Wu, Li, Helleseth and Zhang is proven in odd characteristic”. However, their result on Conjecture 1 were not given in explicit format; see Proposition 3.4 in [8].\n\nOn the other hand, the second conjecture (Conjecture 4.20 in [42]) was proven recently in [24].\n\nTheorem 1\n\n## Theorem 3.2 in [24]\n\nLet p be an odd prime. Let and . Then is a CPP over {\\mathbb{F}}_{{p}^{nk}}, where such that .\n\nWe note that this result is true for prime . However, when is large or not prime, the classification of CPP exponents is still open. For example, when is a power of primes such as 8 or 9, only a few new examples of CPPs are provided in [8]. In this paper, we construct several new classes of complete permutation monomials of {\\mathbb{F}}_{q} using the AGW criterion, when is either a Dickson permutation polynomial or a degree p exceptional polynomial. This provides new applications of AGW criterion besides what have been studied recently in [3], [20], [43], [44], [46]. As consequences, we extend some of previous results on both conjectures. In Section 2, we use Dickson permutation polynomials to construct explicitly some new classes of complete permutation monomials without the restriction of . See Theorem 3 for finite fields with odd characteristic and Corollary 5 for even characteristic. The proofs use factorization results on Dickson polynomials and we explicitly provide the conditions when Conjecture 1 holds for finite fields with arbitrary characteristic (Proposition 1 and Theorem 4). This shows that Conjecture 1 is false in general and a counterexample is also included in this section. In Section 3, we first use the AGW criterion to prove the exceptionality of a well known class of degree p permutation polynomials (Lemma 2). Then we derive two new classes of complete permutation monomials of {\\mathbb{F}}_{{q}^{n}} for general n such that or respectively (see Theorem 5 and Theorem 6). Our results extend Theorem 3.2 in [24]. Indeed, Theorem 1 is a special case of our results when and . Our Theorem 5 deals with more general n such that and more general a's such that when . An extension on CPP exponents for large integers n such that can be found in Theorem 6.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNew results on permutation polynomials over finite fields (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/278733673_New_results_on_permutation_polynomials_over_finite_fields)\nciteturn0search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.5 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; In this paper, four classes of monomial complete permutation polynomials and one class of trinomial complete permutation polynomials are presented, one of which confirms a conjecture proposed by Wu et al. ... Jingxue Ma\n\n## Abstract\n\nPermutation polynomials over finite fields constitute an active research area and have applications in many areas of science and engineering. In this paper, four classes of monomial complete permutation polynomials and one class of trinomial complete permutation polynomials are presented, one of which confirms a conjecture proposed by Wu et al. (Sci. China Math., to appear. Doi: 10.1007/s11425-014-4964-2). Furthermore, we give two classes of trinomial permutation polynomials, and make some progress on a conjecture about the differential uniformity of power permutation polynomials proposed by Blondeau et al. (Int. J. Inf. Coding Theory, 2010, 1, pp. 149-170).\n\nImage: ResearchGate Logo\n\nDiscover the world's research\n\n  * 25+ million members\n  * 160+ million publication pages\n  * 2.3+ billion citations\nJoin for free\n\nPublic Full-text 1\n\nPublisher Preview 1\n\nContent uploaded by Tao Feng\n\nAuthor content\n\nAll content in this area