nce, Dobbertin [8] proved a conjecture\n\nof Welch by constructing a class of permutation trinomials. Ding et al. [7] constructed a family of\n\nskew Hadamard diﬀerence sets v ia the Dickson permutation polynomial of order ﬁve, which dispr oved\n\nthe longstanding conjecture on skew Hadamard diﬀerence sets. Some recent progress on permutation\n\npolynomials can be fou nd in [1, 5, 6, 10, 15, 12, 13, 14, 17, 26, 27, 28].\n\nA polynomial f(x) ∈ F\n\np\n\nn\n\n[x] is called a complete permutation polynomial (CPP) if both f (x) and\n\nf(x)+x are permu tations of F\n\np\n\nn\n\n. These polynomials were introduced by Niederreiter an d Robinson in\n\n∗\n\nCorresponding author. Email address: majingxue@zju.edu.cn (J. Ma), tzh@zju.edu.cn (T. Zhang), tfeng@zju.edu.cn\n\n(T. Feng), gnge@zju.edu.cn (G. Ge)\n\n1\n\n[19]. The simplest polynomials are monomials, and for a positive integer d and α ∈ F\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA general construction of regular complete permutation polynomials (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/366603063_A_general_construction_of_regular_complete_permutation_polynomials)\nciteturn0search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.9 years ago; Crawled: 3 months ago; By Theorem 3.2(2)(4), we have σ\nare both 7-regular CPPs over F q 3.\n\n18\n\nProof. Since p= 2, we have\n\nQ 7(t) = t 6+t 5+t 4+t 3+t 2+t+ 1\n\n= (t 3+t 2+ 1)(t 3+t+ 1)\n\nand\n\n(t+ 1)7−1 = t 7+ 7 t 6+ 21 t 5+ 35 t 4+ 35 t 3+ 21 t 2+ 7 t+ 1 −1\n\n=t 7+t 6+t 5+t 4+t 3+t 2+t=tQ 7(t).\n\nThen h(t) = t 3+t 2+ 1 |(t 7−1) and h(t)|((t+ 1)7−1). By Theorem 3.2(2)(4), we have σ\n\nis a 7-regular CPP over F q 3 and σ+e is a 7-regular PP over F q 3. Meanwhile, (σ+e) + e=σ\n\nis a PP over F q 3. So σ+e is also a 7-regular CPP over F q 3.\n\n\n\nBy the same method used in Proposition 4.6, we can prove\n\nProposition 4.7. Assume that p= 2. Let h(t) = t 3+t+ 1 and M∈M 3×3(F q)such that\n\nP M(t) = h(t). Let τ be any additive PP over F q 3. Let σ=